    "['ipv6 enable', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `ipv6 enable` command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, you need to explicitly enable IPv6 protocol family for each interface. The command is applied per interface unit (with unit 0 being the default). I included both interfaces from the original configuration for completeness.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `ipv6 enable` command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, you need to explicitly enable IPv6 protocol family for each interface. The command is applied per interface unit (with unit 0 being the default). I included both interfaces from the original configuration for completeness.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 address [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:1::1/64', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `ipv6 address` command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:1::1/64\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. \"GigabitEthernet1/0/0\" translates to \"ge-1/0/0\" in Juniper\\n2. Juniper requires specifying a logical unit (here unit 0)\\n3. \"family inet6\" is Juniper\\'s equivalent to \"ipv6\" in Cisco\\n4. The address format remains identical between platforms', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:1::1/64'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 enable', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nNote: The Juniper equivalent enables IPv6 on the interface by configuring the `inet6` family. Since Juniper uses a hierarchical configuration model, we specify this under the interface's unit 0 (default unit) configuration. The translation covers both interfaces (ge-1/0/0 and ge-2/0/0) from the original Cisco configuration.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nNote: The Juniper equivalent enables IPv6 on the interface by configuring the `inet6` family. Since Juniper uses a hierarchical configuration model, we specify this under the interface's unit 0 (default unit) configuration. The translation covers both interfaces (ge-1/0/0 and ge-2/0/0) from the original Cisco configuration.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 address [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:100::1/96', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `ipv6 address 2001:db8:100::1/96` command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:100::1/96\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- `ge-2/0/0` is Juniper's equivalent interface naming for GigabitEthernet2/0/0\\n- `unit 0` is required in Juniper as all interfaces must have at least one logical unit\\n- `family inet6` specifies IPv6 configuration (equivalent to Cisco's `ipv6` prefix)\\n- The address format remains the same (2001:db8:100::1/96)\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:100::1/96'}]]": 1,
