    "['no synchronization', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'no synchronization', 'parent_command': ['set protocols bgp local-as [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set protocols bgp local-as [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nno synchronization\\n##\\n\\nFor Juniper devices, the equivalent command would be to disable BGP synchronization, which is achieved by default in JunOS (BGP synchronization is not enabled by default). However, if you need to explicitly configure it, the Juniper command would be:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols bgp group <group-name> no-synchronize\\n##\\n\\nNote: In the Juniper command, you would need to specify the BGP group name where this should be applied. Since the original Cisco configuration doesn\\'t specify a group name, you might use something like \"EXTERNAL\" or another appropriate name in the Juniper configuration.', 'trans_template': 'no synchronization'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols bgp group <group-name> no-synchronize', 'parent_command': ['set protocols bgp local-as [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set protocols bgp local-as [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nno synchronization\\n##\\n\\nFor Juniper devices, the equivalent command would be to disable BGP synchronization, which is achieved by default in JunOS (BGP synchronization is not enabled by default). However, if you need to explicitly configure it, the Juniper command would be:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols bgp group <group-name> no-synchronize\\n##\\n\\nNote: In the Juniper command, you would need to specify the BGP group name where this should be applied. Since the original Cisco configuration doesn\\'t specify a group name, you might use something like \"EXTERNAL\" or another appropriate name in the Juniper configuration.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols bgp group <group-name> no-synchronize'}]]": 1,
